# Go Web Platform Breaking-Changes Rebranding

## Context

Phase 2 owns every change that can affect imports, generated code, wire
negotiation, authentication, persisted state, automation, database security,
deployed resources, repository coordinates, or rollback. Examples include
`github.com/skaia/*`, protobuf packages, `skaia.proto.v1`, `skaia-api`,
`X-Skaia-API-Key`, browser/Redis namespaces, source/serialized markers,
PostgreSQL roles/triggers, `skaia-backend`, Docker resources, provisioning paths,
systemd configuration, and the Git remote.

This phase begins only after the surface release is verified.

## Goal

- Apply one approved lowercase GWP machine identity across source, protocols,
  persisted state, runtime infrastructure, repository, and releases.
- Support rolling deployment and migration without invalidating sessions,
  resetting enforcement, losing tenant data, or attaching to empty resources.
- End with no unexplained live Skaia identifiers and removal conditions for
  every temporary compatibility alias.

## Non-Goals

- Repeating phase-1 copy/asset work or redesigning the application.
- Renaming Grengo, tenant IDs/domains/content, or SkaiaCraft without approval.
- Rewriting applied SQL or Git history for cosmetic consistency.
- Removing aliases/resources before production proof and the rollback window.

## Steps

1. Freeze the identity matrix: slug, repository and Go coordinates, npm name,
   image/resource prefix, protobuf/RPC/WS names, JWT issuer, API-key header,
   browser/Redis namespaces, source/serialized markers, database objects,
   service paths, and registry/release coordinates. Verify destinations exist.
2. Define compatibility and removal gates: negotiate old/new WS protocols and
   gRPC services; accept the old JWT issuer through maximum token lifetime;
   prefer the new API header while accepting the old; migrate browser/document
   values read-old/write-new; preserve Redis security state; and use additive
   database migrations compatible with partially upgraded services.
3. Rename root/backend/features/gRPC modules and imports coherently. Rename
   protobuf sources/packages, preserve field numbers and wire shapes, regenerate
   Go/TypeScript artifacts, and require clean second generation.
4. Rename internal backend/frontend contracts including auth, protobuf lookup,
   WebRTC, CSS identifiers, datasource globals, page markers, browser/Redis keys,
   package metadata, test identities, logs, and metrics. Add migration adapters
   wherever values can outlive a deployment.
5. Inventory deployed state and build an idempotent infrastructure cutover with
   collision checks, backups, health gates, rollback, and exact mappings for
   images, containers, networks, volumes, databases/roles, systemd, provisioning,
   tenant files, Frappe/Superset, monitoring, and automation.
6. Update Compose, control-plane templates, provisioning, Dockerfiles, services,
   health/metrics, and clean-install defaults. Add forward database migrations
   preserving ownership, grants, and hard-delete enforcement; do not edit history.
7. Test clean install, upgrade, interrupted retry, mixed versions, downgrade,
   rollback, backup/restore, import/transfer, frontend shipping, nginx reload,
   and provisioned-service lifecycles before staged production rollout.
8. Once destinations exist, rename repository/release coordinates and update
   remotes, CI/CD, registries, automation, monitoring, publishing, and developer
   paths. Treat workspace-directory rename as an explicit operator action.
9. Reconcile specs, routines, active todos, operations docs, and generated indexes.
   Classify every residual old-name match as tenant-owned, immutable history, or
   time-bounded compatibility.
10. Observe compatibility through the approved window. Remove aliases/resources
    only in a reviewed release with telemetry, client-version, rollback, and
    state-inventory evidence; then retire the sequence.

## Verification

- Pass full root/backend/features/gRPC tests, vet, static analysis, race coverage,
  builds, and frontend type-check/build/tests/lint/policy gates.
- Test mixed WS/gRPC clients, JWT issuers, API header precedence, browser/document
  migration, Redis continuity, and rollback/downgrade behavior.
- Regenerate protobufs twice; confirm no second diff and stable wire shapes.
- Compare tenant/database/object, volume, upload, provisioning, and service state
  before/after cutover and prove database security during partial/final upgrades.
- Validate install/upgrade/retry/restore/import/ship/nginx/provisioning, observability,
  repository, registry, packages, and public responses.
- Produce a residual-name report with owner, rationale, and removal gate; require
  zero unexplained live or user-facing Skaia identity.
- Run `git diff --check`, validate sequence indexes, update specs, and record
  staging/production evidence.

